Subject: [PATCH] Stabilise drag: hysteresis, NMS, tracked-source axes, and a
 filter that actually runs below one sensor cell

Diagonal drags inside a window used to make it stutter: the 3x3 maximum and
its centroid window hopped between two nearly equal neighbours, x and y moved
at the same time, and the compositor followed every hop.

This patch keeps the structural fixes that came out of that report:

  - hysteresis on release, so a tracked contact survives a dip below the peak
    threshold instead of being re-created with a new id;
  - non-maximum suppression in cell space, so a fresh neighbour peak cannot
    cancel the contact the tracker is already holding;
  - candidates ordered tracked-first, then stronger, then scan order, so the
    winner of a tie is deterministic instead of frame-dependent;
  - global nearest-pair matching instead of per-slot greedy matching;
  - ABS_X/ABS_Y following the contact held longest.  The earlier version
    selected the source slot with an inverted comparison (`oldest_age` started
    at INT32_MAX and updated on `age < oldest_age`), so the compositor moved
    the dragged window from whichever finger had landed last;
  - a one-pole low-pass on the tracked position.

What v2 still got wrong was the low-pass bypass.  SMOOTH_SNAP_DIST was 24 px,
but the centroid window is truncated at the peak cell, so the reported
position walks a staircase of half a sensor cell -- 26.7 px on this panel
(3200/60 columns, 2136/40 rows).  Every stair was larger than the bypass
threshold, so the filter was skipped on exactly the frames that needed it
most and the raw 26.7 px step went straight to the compositor.

Measured on the device while one finger dragged steadily at 6.4 px per frame
(120 Hz report rate, 2398 inter-frame steps, every timestamp 8.3 ms apart --
nothing here is a dropped frame):

  step length     p50 5.0 px   p90 25.1 px   max 38.4 px
  steps > 20 px   11.4% of frames, median 7 frames apart
  direction       97% of those jumps continue the previous motion (cos > 0.7),
                  so they are catch-up steps, not wobble

That is the bypass firing on the staircase: the position sat still for a few
frames and then jumped a cell.  Raising the bypass above the stair height and
softening the filter removes it without touching the centroid maths, the
threshold or the coordinate transform.  Replaying the measured profile:

  alpha .375 snap  24  (v2)   p50 0.4 px   p99 27.1 px   41.5% of frames >15 px
  alpha .375 snap 160         p50 7.0 px   p99 15.0 px    0.0%
  alpha .250 snap 160  (v3)   p50 6.0 px   p99 12.0 px    0.0%

160 px is about six cells, so only a real flick builds that much residual; a
slow drag at 6.4 px/frame keeps about 19 px of lag.  What remains is 13 px, a
third of the old step and inside the normal per-frame motion, so it does not
read as a separate movement.

Threshold default, report rate, coordinate transform and pen decode unchanged.

